        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(qa): enforce surface.feature coverage IDs",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nResolves two related QA coverage identity problems. First, 158 semantic IDs were assigned to multiple taxonomy features, so reports could not identify one exact owner. Second, collision-qualified IDs could grow to three dot-separated parts even though the implementation performs exact string matching, making IDs such as `surface.feature.context` look hierarchical when they were not.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nCoverage IDs now have one canonical shape: `<surface-id>.<feature-id>`. Every taxonomy feature owns exactly one globally unique ID, and that ID's first segment must match its containing taxonomy surface. Broad rows that previously owned multiple semantic claims are split into atomic feature rows, so scenario YAML can reference only the exact feature its executable proves.\n\nThis keeps one vocabulary and one ownership path: taxonomy defines semantic IDs; scenario YAML maps executables to those IDs. There are no aliases, fallback readers, compatibility names, TypeScript eligibility registries, or implied parent/child coverage. A scenario covering `surface.feature-context` does not cover `surface.feature` unless it explicitly lists both exact IDs.\n\nThe original collision migration and its profile reconciliation remain part of the same atomic change. The fixed 12-scenario CI smoke plan remains unchanged, while `smoke-ci` narrows from 30 categories to the 11 exact categories that plan proves.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo OpenClaw runtime behavior changes. Maintainers and QA operators get unambiguous, flat coverage identities; truthful scenario-to-feature evidence; and selectors/profiles that reflect executable product proof. Internal QA coverage references are renamed to the strict two-part contract.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Original reconciliation: 1,992 taxonomy assignments before and after; 158 colliding source IDs across 479 assignments; 321 excess assignments migrated; all 395 previously affected scenario occurrences re-evaluated; zero unresolved/high-risk rows, duplicate targets, unknown targets, new last-primary losses, or zero-coverage scenarios.\n- Historical strict normalization: 1,676 broad feature rows -> 1,992 atomic feature rows; 92 multi-ID features -> 0; 260 three-part IDs -> 0; 1,461 wrong-surface prefixes -> 0; 1,717 IDs renamed and 275 already canonical.\n- Reconciliation against current `main`: 1,998 taxonomy features/assignments, 1,998 globally unique IDs, zero multi-ID features, zero non-two-part IDs, and zero surface-prefix mismatches. The current catalog has 356 scenarios and 571 exact references with zero unknown references.\n- Current-main reconciliation preserves newly landed cron, memory, UI, `ask_user`, reconnect, and sender-policy scenarios/features. The strengthened reconnect executable claims exact reconnect, dedup, and delivery owners. `ask_user` claims exact Tool-call handling and Gateway Chat API owners. The two sender-policy live probes now primarily claim exact delegated-tool access; their secondary claims are limited to proven subagent turns, dormant completion delivery, and the unpredictable filesystem-read marker. Generic QA-channel inheritance was dropped where the channel is only the harness transport.\n- Review also corrected the dreaming shadow-trial mapping to the exact Memory Files owner and split WhatsApp outbound media into exact image, document, audio/voice-note, and video features; scenarios claim only the variants their executable asserts.\n- `smoke-ci` is intentionally 30 -> 11 categories. The exact Memory category is required because the fixed plan explicitly runs the dreaming shadow-trial scenario; adding it makes 10 current memory scenarios profile-eligible but does not expand the bounded automatic 12-scenario plan. A timeless test now requires every fixed smoke scenario to belong to a selected profile category and every selected category to have fixed-plan evidence.\n- Ignored detailed reconciliation JSON SHA-256: `b8ee2ee1264a26eb4d08d312f82504906394ebe570a9683e0bbcb32f696d3aea` (base `33720886c8a8aa948c03803f463266a68ba956e0`, head `6521671e8b1a02cbd444155488fa6cb79e7de5ff`). The detailed JSON remains under `tmp`; no migration inventory is tracked.\n- Blacksmith Testbox rebase proof: full formatting and 178/178 focused tests passed (88 workflow guards plus 90 QA taxonomy/catalog/smoke/evidence/runner tests). The broad changed gate passed all repository guards and full-project tsgo; its sole task finding was the catalog max-lines ratchet. The final owning-test compaction then passed full formatting, focused lint/max-lines, and 46/46 catalog tests: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29786903155 and https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29788511188.\n- Latest current-main proof on supported Node 24 passed full formatting plus 164 focused checks: 89 workflow guards and 75 catalog/coverage-report/scorecard/smoke-plan tests. The catalog-only precursor passed 46/46: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29791265782 and https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29791049514.\n- Fresh Codex autoreview used `gpt-5.6-sol` with high reasoning. The complete non-sensitive 976,288-byte branch bundle ran in three bounded passes; all returned zero findings and `patch is correct` (merged confidence 0.90). A fresh review of the final two-scenario current-main delta also returned zero findings and `patch is correct` (confidence 0.83). The helper excluded only four sensitive-filename YAML diffs already covered by the prior clean same-model review; those mappings are unchanged and present in the final diff.\n- Successor exact-head CI is running for `6521671e8b1a02cbd444155488fa6cb79e7de5ff`: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29791400943. Exact-head ClawSweeper dispatch succeeded: https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29791398720.\n- No migration-named test/artifact or historical-count registry is tracked. Existing owning tests enforce global uniqueness, known-reference integrity, exact two-part shape, one ID per feature, surface ownership, role constraints, and current smoke/profile behavior.\n- AI-assisted; I inspected the affected taxonomy, catalog, selectors, scorecard/report projections, evidence producers, tests, and the previously required sibling Codex protocol boundaries, and I understand the resulting mappings.\n",
